1
00:00:00,089 --> 00:00:02,132
[Reacher] <i>Grew up in the military,
worked in the military.</i>

2
00:00:02,133 --> 00:00:03,883
<i>Always told where to go, when to be there.</i>

3
00:00:03,884 --> 00:00:05,510
<i>I wanted to see my country
on my own terms.</i>

4
00:00:05,511 --> 00:00:08,513
<i>Motels, cash, fake names, travel light.</i>

5
00:00:08,514 --> 00:00:11,266
<i>Mostly, I take buses or hitchhike.
You get used to it.</i>

6
00:00:11,267 --> 00:00:14,144
[Finlay] <i>Command at
the 110th Special Investigations Unit</i>

7
00:00:14,145 --> 00:00:15,562
<i>of the Military Police.</i>

8
00:00:15,563 --> 00:00:17,480
[Neagley] <i>It's not just 'cause
you need to put things right.</i>

9
00:00:17,481 --> 00:00:19,065
<i>And it's not because
you love the little guy.</i>

10
00:00:19,066 --> 00:00:20,442
It's 'cause you hate the big guy.

11
00:00:20,443 --> 00:00:22,569
<i>You hate the smug sons of bitches</i>

12
00:00:22,570 --> 00:00:24,362
<i>who think they can just
get away with things.</i>

13
00:00:24,363 --> 00:00:25,947
You just figured that out?

14
00:00:25,948 --> 00:00:27,742
[energetic rock music playing]

15
00:00:28,325 --> 00:00:30,745
[grunting]

16
00:00:33,956 --> 00:00:35,708
[shotgun blast]

17
00:00:37,042 --> 00:00:38,251
[Reacher] <i>Hundred thousand years ago,</i>

18
00:00:38,252 --> 00:00:40,086
<i>there were people who stayed
by the campfire,</i>

19
00:00:40,087 --> 00:00:41,630
<i>and people who wandered.</i>

20
00:00:42,923 --> 00:00:46,551
<i>I'm pretty sure I'm a direct descendant
of the wandering type.</i>

21
00:00:46,552 --> 00:00:48,345
<i>It's just who I am.</i>

22
00:00:58,856 --> 00:01:00,858
[♪ Hop Wilson plays "Chicken Stuff"]

23
00:01:15,873 --> 00:01:16,999
{\an8}<i>♪ Chicken stuff ♪</i>

24
00:01:21,796 --> 00:01:23,798
[indistinct chatter]

25
00:01:33,349 --> 00:01:34,517
[food sizzling]

26
00:01:38,771 --> 00:01:40,396
Whiz wit.

27
00:01:40,397 --> 00:01:41,523
[distant horns honking]

28
00:01:41,524 --> 00:01:42,983
[crickets chirping]

29
00:01:49,740 --> 00:01:51,534
[train rattling on tracks]

30
00:01:57,957 --> 00:01:59,499
[P.A. chimes]

31
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:01,501
[P.A. announcer] <i>Northbound train
now boarding.</i>

32
00:02:01,502 --> 00:02:03,670
<i>Service continues to Ellsworth-Federal,</i>

33
00:02:03,671 --> 00:02:06,548
<i>Spring Garden, City Hall and beyond.</i>

34
00:02:06,549 --> 00:02:08,092
[man shouts indistinctly]

35
00:02:11,053 --> 00:02:12,304
[rat squeaking]

36
00:02:22,481 --> 00:02:24,483
[train approaching]

37
00:02:56,140 --> 00:02:59,351
[woman crying]

38
00:03:03,439 --> 00:03:05,733
[quietly muttering]

39
00:03:16,118 --> 00:03:18,120
[dramatic music playing]

40
00:03:25,169 --> 00:03:27,171
[muttering]

41
00:03:36,388 --> 00:03:38,349
I need to see your hands.

42
00:03:42,436 --> 00:03:44,146
May I sit next to you?

43
00:03:45,230 --> 00:03:46,481
Leave me alone.

44
00:03:46,482 --> 00:03:48,150
I can't do that.

45
00:03:50,819 --> 00:03:52,236
Go away.

46
00:03:52,237 --> 00:03:54,198
There's only a few people on the train.

47
00:03:56,200 --> 00:03:58,869
If you waited till rush hour,
your plan would be more effective.

48
00:04:02,081 --> 00:04:04,875
Just... one hand.

49
00:04:07,294 --> 00:04:08,462
Just take out one hand.

50
00:04:09,922 --> 00:04:11,255
I can't.

51
00:04:11,256 --> 00:04:12,799
[Reacher] Yes, you can.

52
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:15,051
What you have in there is very dangerous.

53
00:04:15,052 --> 00:04:17,221
The less contact
you have with it, the better.

54
00:04:18,347 --> 00:04:19,598
Easy.

55
00:04:20,849 --> 00:04:22,892
Don't do anything you can't undo.

56
00:04:22,893 --> 00:04:24,686
[crying softly]

57
00:04:24,687 --> 00:04:26,188
Show me one hand.

58
00:04:28,857 --> 00:04:29,900
Please.

59
00:04:32,861 --> 00:04:34,863
[suspenseful music playing]

60
00:04:40,494 --> 00:04:42,246
Thank you.

61
00:04:46,458 --> 00:04:48,002
Now...

62
00:04:49,878 --> 00:04:51,005
...show me what you have.

63
00:04:52,464 --> 00:04:53,631
I need to see it.

64
00:04:53,632 --> 00:04:54,883
Please.

65
00:04:56,844 --> 00:04:59,012
[sniffles] Go away.

66
00:04:59,013 --> 00:05:00,388
I can't do that.

67
00:05:00,389 --> 00:05:02,807
- Why?
- Because I'm involved now.

68
00:05:02,808 --> 00:05:04,643
Whatever you're in, I'm in it now, too.

69
00:05:06,603 --> 00:05:07,729
Just...

70
00:05:07,730 --> 00:05:10,232
take it out of the bag.

71
00:05:12,109 --> 00:05:13,777
♪ ♪

72
00:05:16,196 --> 00:05:18,032
That's better than
the bomb I thought you had.

73
00:05:19,533 --> 00:05:21,325
You don't have a problem with me.

74
00:05:21,326 --> 00:05:22,786
You don't even know me.

75
00:05:25,497 --> 00:05:27,790
[crying] It's over.

76
00:05:27,791 --> 00:05:29,293
It's all over.

77
00:05:29,918 --> 00:05:31,045
What is?

78
00:05:32,713 --> 00:05:33,839
Just talk to me.

79
00:05:36,925 --> 00:05:38,177
I can help you.

80
00:05:39,928 --> 00:05:41,804
I'm a cop.

81
00:05:41,805 --> 00:05:43,807
[ominous music playing]

82
00:05:47,102 --> 00:05:48,520
You're a cop?

83
00:05:52,691 --> 00:05:54,650
No one can help me now.

84
00:05:54,651 --> 00:05:56,820
- [passenger screams]
- [man] Oh, shit, man!

85
00:06:06,789 --> 00:06:09,416
[thunder rumbling]

86
00:06:22,054 --> 00:06:24,056
[speaking indistinctly]

87
00:06:30,521 --> 00:06:33,148
[distant siren wailing]

88
00:06:37,194 --> 00:06:39,654
Mr. Reacher, Detective Shaun Docherty.

89
00:06:39,655 --> 00:06:41,948
This is my partner Detective Tamara Green.

90
00:06:41,949 --> 00:06:44,117
We have a few questions for you.

91
00:06:44,118 --> 00:06:47,328
Shouldn't take too long if you're honest
and cooperative.

92
00:06:47,329 --> 00:06:48,705
Easy enough, right?

93
00:06:53,377 --> 00:06:55,753
Right? So...

94
00:06:55,754 --> 00:06:57,255
Okay, you, uh,

95
00:06:57,256 --> 00:07:00,092
you had a passport on you,
but no driver's license.

96
00:07:02,886 --> 00:07:03,970
No answer for that?

97
00:07:03,971 --> 00:07:05,430
It wasn't a question.

98
00:07:08,308 --> 00:07:09,767
Where do you currently live?

99
00:07:09,768 --> 00:07:11,352
What's the address of this building?

100
00:07:11,353 --> 00:07:13,563
- Why?
- It's where I'm currently living.

101
00:07:13,564 --> 00:07:14,939
Where's your home, asshole?

102
00:07:14,940 --> 00:07:15,940
Don't have one.

103
00:07:15,941 --> 00:07:17,275
So you're homeless.

104
00:07:17,276 --> 00:07:19,026
Now I see why you're a detective.

105
00:07:19,027 --> 00:07:20,736
Motherfucker!

106
00:07:20,737 --> 00:07:23,406
You think I haven't
dealt with wiseasses before?

107
00:07:23,407 --> 00:07:26,409
Okay, okay. Let's try this.

108
00:07:26,410 --> 00:07:28,202
Where were you before the subway, Reacher?

109
00:07:28,203 --> 00:07:29,620
[Reacher] The Uptown on Frankfort.

110
00:07:29,621 --> 00:07:30,621
Listening to music.

111
00:07:30,622 --> 00:07:32,165
Then you went straight to the subway.

112
00:07:32,166 --> 00:07:34,167
No. Then, I got a cheesesteak.

113
00:07:34,168 --> 00:07:35,626
You ate a cheesesteak at 2:00 a.m.?

114
00:07:35,627 --> 00:07:37,629
- I'm in Philly.
- Why?

115
00:07:38,463 --> 00:07:39,547
'Cause they taste good.

116
00:07:39,548 --> 00:07:41,632
Why-- Jesus tap-dancing Christ.

117
00:07:41,633 --> 00:07:43,843
Why are you in Philadelphia?
'Cause you don't sound like

118
00:07:43,844 --> 00:07:45,428
- you're from here.
- Phillies have a kid

119
00:07:45,429 --> 00:07:47,388
who consistently throws over 100.

120
00:07:47,389 --> 00:07:49,432
Wanted to see him play
before he blows his arm out.

121
00:07:49,433 --> 00:07:51,726
Oh, but instead,
you blow a woman's brains out.

122
00:07:51,727 --> 00:07:54,687
I didn't kill her. I don't even know her.

123
00:07:54,688 --> 00:07:56,856
The only reason I approached her
is 'cause she had several

124
00:07:56,857 --> 00:07:58,774
of the behavioral indicators
of a suicide bomber.

125
00:07:58,775 --> 00:08:00,026
Like what?

126
00:08:00,027 --> 00:08:01,277
You don't know them?

127
00:08:01,278 --> 00:08:03,529
[Docherty] Since 9/11,
every city cop in America knows them.

128
00:08:03,530 --> 00:08:06,115
How do you know them?
Were they trivia questions

129
00:08:06,116 --> 00:08:07,700
on the back of a creatine box?

130
00:08:07,701 --> 00:08:08,619
I was an MP.

131
00:08:09,620 --> 00:08:11,454
And I saw a nervous woman, sweating,

132
00:08:11,455 --> 00:08:13,706
fidgeting, mumbling, possibly praying,
looking straight ahead.

133
00:08:13,707 --> 00:08:15,917
So you don't have to see
the people you're about to kill.

134
00:08:15,918 --> 00:08:17,460
Wearing a heavy coat for the time
of year to hide explosives.

135
00:08:17,461 --> 00:08:20,338
Had a large bag.
She was checking all the boxes.

136
00:08:20,339 --> 00:08:24,091
Including the one
where she kills nobody except herself.

137
00:08:24,092 --> 00:08:25,468
You know what I think?

138
00:08:25,469 --> 00:08:26,678
Stupid things?

139
00:08:28,222 --> 00:08:29,764
I think you had something to do with it.

140
00:08:29,765 --> 00:08:32,350
There were witnesses
on the train. Ask them.

141
00:08:32,351 --> 00:08:34,310
- They saw what happened.
- [Docherty] We did. They didn't.

142
00:08:34,311 --> 00:08:36,187
Some of them were asleep,
others weren't looking.

143
00:08:36,188 --> 00:08:38,064
Four witnesses and no one saw anything?

144
00:08:38,065 --> 00:08:40,107
- Three.
- There were four.

145
00:08:40,108 --> 00:08:43,527
A small Hispanic woman in her 40s,
Black man in his 30s,

146
00:08:43,528 --> 00:08:45,321
both tired, both coming off a night shift.

147
00:08:45,322 --> 00:08:46,822
Balkan immigrant, mid-20s.

148
00:08:46,823 --> 00:08:48,616
How the hell do you know
he was a Balkan immigrant?

149
00:08:48,617 --> 00:08:50,785
Coloring, height.

150
00:08:50,786 --> 00:08:53,037
People from that region
are taller on average.

151
00:08:53,038 --> 00:08:55,915
He was putting ketchup on his pizza,
which originated in Albania,

152
00:08:55,916 --> 00:08:59,085
and he was wearing a Colorado State
shirt that was spelled with all "O's."

153
00:08:59,086 --> 00:09:01,879
Irregular shirts are given to charities,
and he was wearing whatever

154
00:09:01,880 --> 00:09:04,298
hand-out fit. So, Balkan.

155
00:09:04,299 --> 00:09:05,884
Immigrant.

156
00:09:06,510 --> 00:09:09,512
Okay, Sherlock Has-No-Holmes,
that's three. You said four.

157
00:09:09,513 --> 00:09:11,514
Caucasian male. Mid-50s.

158
00:09:11,515 --> 00:09:13,891
Far end of the car. Tan pants. Golf shirt.

159
00:09:13,892 --> 00:09:15,185
There was no one there like that.

160
00:09:15,811 --> 00:09:16,978
Then he took off in the confusion.

161
00:09:16,979 --> 00:09:19,897
Guns, dead bodies, lots of reasons to run.

162
00:09:19,898 --> 00:09:21,691
Maybe he told his wife
he was away on business,

163
00:09:21,692 --> 00:09:23,859
but he was taking the train
to see his girlfriend.

164
00:09:23,860 --> 00:09:26,321
I don't know. Who gives a shit?
But he was there.

165
00:09:26,989 --> 00:09:29,073
Did you kill Anna Merrick?
Was that your gun?

166
00:09:29,074 --> 00:09:31,200
What is this, good cop, dumb cop?

167
00:09:31,201 --> 00:09:33,244
She was killed with a .357 Magnum.

168
00:09:33,245 --> 00:09:35,913
I don't have a drop of blood on me
and I'd have gun residue up to my wrist.

169
00:09:35,914 --> 00:09:37,832
But when I got here,
you followed me into the bathroom

170
00:09:37,833 --> 00:09:39,417
and let me wash my hands.

171
00:09:39,418 --> 00:09:41,627
You wouldn't have done that
if you thought I shot this woman.

172
00:09:41,628 --> 00:09:43,463
You would have fingerprinted
and mirandized me.

173
00:09:45,590 --> 00:09:46,591
You're stalling.

174
00:09:54,516 --> 00:09:57,435
You're right. We're holding onto you
because we got a call

175
00:09:57,436 --> 00:09:59,312
some other people want
to ask you some questions.

176
00:09:59,313 --> 00:10:00,938
Feds from D.C.

177
00:10:00,939 --> 00:10:02,231
How do you know that?

178
00:10:02,232 --> 00:10:04,150
If it was FBI,
they'd be questioning me already

179
00:10:04,151 --> 00:10:05,819
'cause they have a branch office
here downtown.

180
00:10:07,279 --> 00:10:08,571
It's got to be folks up from D.C.

181
00:10:08,572 --> 00:10:12,409
CIA, DEA, DOD,
anyone else from the alphabet gang.

182
00:10:13,493 --> 00:10:15,745
Turns out our suicide worked
for the federal government.

183
00:10:15,746 --> 00:10:18,497
Somehow, that makes this all
a bit more complicated,

184
00:10:18,498 --> 00:10:21,167
so I'm asking you nicely,
if you could stay a while longer

185
00:10:21,168 --> 00:10:23,419
until they get here
and things are cleared up.

186
00:10:23,420 --> 00:10:25,713
[tense music playing]

187
00:10:25,714 --> 00:10:27,423
I'll stay because
the Feds might know something

188
00:10:27,424 --> 00:10:29,133
about why she killed herself,
and I want to know.

189
00:10:29,134 --> 00:10:30,801
[Docherty] You said
you didn't know the victim.

190
00:10:30,802 --> 00:10:32,428
Why do you care?

191
00:10:32,429 --> 00:10:36,182
'Cause she was desperate
and needed help...

192
00:10:36,183 --> 00:10:37,309
and I didn't help her.

193
00:10:39,519 --> 00:10:42,229
Okay. So we wait a while.

194
00:10:42,230 --> 00:10:44,440
As for all this, you were a cop.

195
00:10:44,441 --> 00:10:45,816
You know we had to poke around,

196
00:10:45,817 --> 00:10:48,361
even though we know
you had nothing to do with what happened.

197
00:10:48,362 --> 00:10:52,365
Well, yeah, maybe not directly, but...

198
00:10:52,366 --> 00:10:54,825
That lady wasn't doing anything
until you ran up on her,

199
00:10:54,826 --> 00:10:57,870
got her all worked up enough
to off herself.

200
00:10:57,871 --> 00:11:00,331
Seen a few mass-transit suicides.

201
00:11:00,332 --> 00:11:01,999
People jump in front of trains.

202
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,627
Not one has popped their top
on one of 'em.

203
00:11:04,628 --> 00:11:08,422
Sometimes people on the edge
get pushed over that edge.

204
00:11:08,423 --> 00:11:10,466
Maybe you were the pusher.

205
00:11:10,467 --> 00:11:13,303
And you'll stay here
because I'm telling you to stay.

206
00:11:14,346 --> 00:11:16,348
You really think
you can make me do anything?

207
00:11:17,641 --> 00:11:19,684
[tense music playing]

208
00:11:30,445 --> 00:11:31,612
[Reacher] Onions, peppers,

209
00:11:31,613 --> 00:11:33,906
- extra cheese.
- For what?

210
00:11:33,907 --> 00:11:35,574
My cheesesteak.

211
00:11:35,575 --> 00:11:36,867
It's gonna be a while.

212
00:11:36,868 --> 00:11:38,954
It's almost 5:00 a.m.

213
00:11:40,038 --> 00:11:41,039
I'm in Philly.

214
00:11:49,840 --> 00:11:51,215
It's not a bad breakfast.

215
00:11:51,216 --> 00:11:52,384
Told ya.

216
00:11:55,804 --> 00:11:57,012
What the hell is that?

217
00:11:57,013 --> 00:11:58,056
That's Gritty.

218
00:12:00,142 --> 00:12:01,268
Flyers mascot?

219
00:12:02,686 --> 00:12:04,520
- Not a hockey guy.
- Missing out.

220
00:12:04,521 --> 00:12:06,605
Perfect blend of speed,
skill and physicality.

221
00:12:06,606 --> 00:12:08,941
Two guys got beef, they battle it out.

222
00:12:08,942 --> 00:12:10,443
No politics, no bullshit.

223
00:12:10,444 --> 00:12:12,695
Well...

224
00:12:12,696 --> 00:12:13,905
some bullshit.

225
00:12:16,408 --> 00:12:17,826
Don't be shitty to my Gritty.

226
00:12:21,079 --> 00:12:22,413
So who'd you piss off?

227
00:12:22,414 --> 00:12:24,457
- What do you mean?
- Well, you're smart.

228
00:12:24,458 --> 00:12:26,167
Clearly smarter than your partner,

229
00:12:26,168 --> 00:12:29,211
but you're stuck on the night shift,
tasked with suicides.

230
00:12:29,212 --> 00:12:31,006
That's incident reports, not police work.

231
00:12:32,424 --> 00:12:33,800
So, who'd you piss off?

232
00:12:35,427 --> 00:12:37,052
Mind your business.

233
00:12:37,053 --> 00:12:39,097
[door opens]

234
00:12:43,560 --> 00:12:46,103
That's Anna's brother Jacob.

235
00:12:46,104 --> 00:12:47,606
Cop from Jersey.

236
00:12:48,440 --> 00:12:50,442
He's carrying himself
like someone still in shock.

237
00:12:51,193 --> 00:12:53,486
Shoulders down and rolled forward, slouched.

238
00:12:53,487 --> 00:12:55,530
The primal instinct to protect yourself.

239
00:12:56,781 --> 00:12:57,990
It doesn't work.

240
00:12:57,991 --> 00:12:59,993
[phone buzzes]

241
00:13:03,330 --> 00:13:05,039
Feds will be here in a couple of minutes.

242
00:13:05,040 --> 00:13:06,374
Finish up.

243
00:13:17,802 --> 00:13:18,803
[clears throat]

244
00:13:28,855 --> 00:13:31,565
You don't seem very concerned
that we're here to question you.

245
00:13:31,566 --> 00:13:33,859
I was already questioned by Philly P.D.

246
00:13:33,860 --> 00:13:35,486
Did pretty well.

247
00:13:35,487 --> 00:13:37,489
Figured I'll only do better this time.

248
00:13:38,573 --> 00:13:39,991
So what agency you fellas with?

249
00:13:42,786 --> 00:13:44,079
What are your names?

250
00:13:49,709 --> 00:13:51,126
Okay.

251
00:13:51,127 --> 00:13:53,171
I'll call you Snap, Crackle and Pop.

252
00:13:53,880 --> 00:13:55,881
So what drags a bunch of B-Team boys
out to Philly?

253
00:13:55,882 --> 00:13:57,633
What makes you think we're the B-Team?

254
00:13:57,634 --> 00:14:00,261
Pulled out of bed to drive almost
three hours in the middle of the night

255
00:14:00,262 --> 00:14:02,471
to question somebody
you know wasn't involved in a shooting

256
00:14:02,472 --> 00:14:03,973
'cause city cops already filled you in.

257
00:14:03,974 --> 00:14:06,600
You're here
so your bosses can put a little check

258
00:14:06,601 --> 00:14:10,688
in a little box that says you interviewed
the last person to talk to Anna Merrick.

259
00:14:10,689 --> 00:14:13,650
If you had the juice to say no,
you'd still be under your quilts.

260
00:14:15,819 --> 00:14:17,028
B-Team.

261
00:14:18,989 --> 00:14:20,448
Maybe C.

262
00:14:22,117 --> 00:14:24,076
So, I want to know
why the government's so interested

263
00:14:24,077 --> 00:14:25,953
in one of its employees killing herself.

264
00:14:25,954 --> 00:14:30,374
The way this works, see,
is you answer our questions.

265
00:14:30,375 --> 00:14:32,042
Why did you talk to Anna Merrick?

266
00:14:32,043 --> 00:14:33,587
I thought she was gonna blow up the train.

267
00:14:34,671 --> 00:14:37,172
You were military, Mr. Reacher,
highly decorated,

268
00:14:37,173 --> 00:14:39,968
but you left on not the best of terms.

269
00:14:40,969 --> 00:14:43,013
Any issues with the Army
or the government?

270
00:14:43,680 --> 00:14:45,139
Tons.

271
00:14:45,140 --> 00:14:46,516
Enough to do something about it?

272
00:14:47,475 --> 00:14:49,561
- Like what?
- Treason.

273
00:14:51,021 --> 00:14:52,564
Not really my style.

274
00:14:55,734 --> 00:14:57,402
Did you receive anything from Anna?

275
00:14:58,486 --> 00:14:59,778
Receive what?

276
00:14:59,779 --> 00:15:02,239
Maybe she gave you something,
and you ditched it,

277
00:15:02,240 --> 00:15:03,908
gonna go back for it later.

278
00:15:04,576 --> 00:15:07,995
Look, Crackle,
I was searched on the platform,

279
00:15:07,996 --> 00:15:09,997
and by now,
you've already picked the subway clean.

280
00:15:09,998 --> 00:15:12,416
Checked the platform,
looked under the seats,

281
00:15:12,417 --> 00:15:14,084
found nothing but gum and boogers.

282
00:15:14,085 --> 00:15:16,462
If I ditched anything on the train,
you would have found it by now.

283
00:15:16,463 --> 00:15:19,466
What is it the government's so worried
she might've given me?

284
00:15:21,843 --> 00:15:24,386
- What are you looking for?
- [Snap] Once again,

285
00:15:24,387 --> 00:15:26,180
we are here to ask the questions.

286
00:15:26,181 --> 00:15:27,765
You're here to answer them.

287
00:15:27,766 --> 00:15:29,267
Not anymore, Snap.

288
00:15:37,776 --> 00:15:39,818
Brought in for murder,
out with no charges.

289
00:15:39,819 --> 00:15:41,695
Better day than I had.

290
00:15:41,696 --> 00:15:44,239
Not my first time, won't be my last.

291
00:15:44,240 --> 00:15:45,950
Take care, Tamara.

292
00:15:47,202 --> 00:15:49,204
Yup.

293
00:15:53,458 --> 00:15:55,752
[vehicle approaching]

294
00:16:07,847 --> 00:16:10,266
May I have a moment of your time?

295
00:16:11,267 --> 00:16:13,268
You just took a moment of my time.

296
00:16:13,269 --> 00:16:16,563
The woman who died in the subway,
did she give you anything?

297
00:16:16,564 --> 00:16:18,691
So far, nothing but hassle.

298
00:16:18,692 --> 00:16:20,442
Did you give it to the police?

299
00:16:20,443 --> 00:16:21,986
Give what to the police?

300
00:16:23,363 --> 00:16:25,322
The flash drive.

301
00:16:25,323 --> 00:16:27,659
I know messenger boys when I see 'em.

302
00:16:28,868 --> 00:16:31,788
So, who's your boss?

303
00:16:32,580 --> 00:16:34,123
[speaking Indonesian]

304
00:16:34,124 --> 00:16:35,874
- [Reacher grunts]
- [man groans]

305
00:16:35,875 --> 00:16:37,335
[Reacher grunts]

306
00:16:38,420 --> 00:16:41,797
[man groaning]

307
00:16:41,798 --> 00:16:45,093
[man coughing, spits]

308
00:16:56,771 --> 00:16:57,938
[♪ Wynonie Harris
sings "Shake That Thing"]

309
00:16:57,939 --> 00:17:01,358
<i>♪ Well, down in Georgia,
they got a dance that's new ♪</i>

310
00:17:01,359 --> 00:17:03,485
<i>♪ Ain't nothin' to it,
'cause it's easy to do ♪</i>

311
00:17:03,486 --> 00:17:04,987
Counter if it's just coffee.

312
00:17:04,988 --> 00:17:07,406
<i>♪ Called "Shake That Thing" ♪</i>

313
00:17:07,407 --> 00:17:10,743
<i>♪ Oh, shake that thing ♪</i>

314
00:17:10,744 --> 00:17:12,953
<i>♪ I'm gettin' sick and tired</i>...

315
00:17:12,954 --> 00:17:15,122
Jacob?

316
00:17:15,123 --> 00:17:16,541
Yeah.

317
00:17:18,918 --> 00:17:20,461
Do I know you?

318
00:17:20,462 --> 00:17:22,629
No, but I'm the last person
to talk to your sister.

319
00:17:22,630 --> 00:17:25,174
I'm a former cop. I can help.

320
00:17:25,175 --> 00:17:26,925
<i>♪ Shake that thing</i>...

321
00:17:26,926 --> 00:17:29,636
[waitress] You decided?

322
00:17:29,637 --> 00:17:32,681
I-I think I'm... I'm good with coffee.

323
00:17:32,682 --> 00:17:34,683
Counter if it's just coffee.

324
00:17:34,684 --> 00:17:36,770
<i>♪ Telling you how to shake that thing ♪</i>

325
00:17:38,938 --> 00:17:41,356
<i>♪ Well, Grandpapa Johnson
grabbed sister Kate...</i>

326
00:17:41,357 --> 00:17:43,942
We won't be long.

327
00:17:43,943 --> 00:17:45,652
[bell dings]

328
00:17:45,653 --> 00:17:47,279
<i>♪ They were shaking that thing</i>...

329
00:17:47,280 --> 00:17:48,989
Do you have any idea what happened?

330
00:17:48,990 --> 00:17:51,576
I only know what I saw.
A distraught woman.

331
00:17:52,744 --> 00:17:55,204
Two men outside jumped me
for some drive Anna was supposed to have.

332
00:17:55,205 --> 00:17:56,914
You know anything about that?

333
00:17:56,915 --> 00:17:58,624
- Jumped you?
- Yeah.

334
00:17:58,625 --> 00:18:00,083
For a drive.

335
00:18:00,084 --> 00:18:03,086
Like a... like a thumb drive?

336
00:18:03,087 --> 00:18:04,922
- Apparently.
- No.

337
00:18:04,923 --> 00:18:08,008
No, I-I don't know anything about it. I...

338
00:18:08,009 --> 00:18:12,179
I just know that there is no way
that my sister killed herself.

339
00:18:12,180 --> 00:18:13,472
I saw her do it.

340
00:18:13,473 --> 00:18:15,475
I don't care what you saw.

341
00:18:16,601 --> 00:18:18,603
She was a happy person.

342
00:18:19,813 --> 00:18:21,897
She loved her job,

343
00:18:21,898 --> 00:18:24,859
she... loved my nephew Ben.

344
00:18:27,570 --> 00:18:29,322
Everything...

345
00:18:30,365 --> 00:18:31,865
Everything was perfect.

346
00:18:31,866 --> 00:18:34,284
She didn't look
like everything was perfect.

347
00:18:34,285 --> 00:18:35,995
She looked terrified.

348
00:18:36,871 --> 00:18:38,163
None of this makes any sense.

349
00:18:38,164 --> 00:18:40,791
You know why
she was wearing a winter coat in summer?

350
00:18:40,792 --> 00:18:43,210
No. I-I-I... I don't know.

351
00:18:43,211 --> 00:18:45,337
Maybe, um...

352
00:18:45,338 --> 00:18:46,839
Maybe she hid the gun in it.

353
00:18:46,840 --> 00:18:48,924
Gun was in the bag.

354
00:18:48,925 --> 00:18:50,843
You know where she got the firearm?

355
00:18:50,844 --> 00:18:53,554
Before our dad died, he gave it to her.

356
00:18:53,555 --> 00:18:57,182
Single woman living alone.
You know, he worried.

357
00:18:57,183 --> 00:18:59,602
- Ben's father?
- That asshole's dead.

358
00:19:00,520 --> 00:19:03,730
The prick took off as soon
as the test showed two pink lines.

359
00:19:03,731 --> 00:19:07,234
A few months later,
he wrapped his car around a tree

360
00:19:07,235 --> 00:19:09,987
with half a bottle
of Johnnie Walker in him.

361
00:19:09,988 --> 00:19:11,864
You know, I helped raise my nephew.

362
00:19:11,865 --> 00:19:13,866
[♪ Big Joe Turner sings
"Morning, Noon and Night"]

363
00:19:13,867 --> 00:19:16,244
Paid for his braces
when things were tough.

364
00:19:17,829 --> 00:19:19,664
Bought him his first football.

365
00:19:20,832 --> 00:19:23,001
He's a linebacker at, uh, Temple.

366
00:19:24,419 --> 00:19:26,712
Full ride.

367
00:19:26,713 --> 00:19:29,214
<i>♪ Morning, noon and night...</i>

368
00:19:29,215 --> 00:19:31,592
I don't know how I'm gonna tell him.

369
00:19:31,593 --> 00:19:33,343
He doesn't know his mother's dead?

370
00:19:33,344 --> 00:19:35,053
No, I can't get ahold of him.

371
00:19:35,054 --> 00:19:36,848
That normal?

372
00:19:38,892 --> 00:19:42,185
I guess. I mean, he's...
he's a 20-year-old kid.

373
00:19:42,186 --> 00:19:43,979
You know, he's got better things
to do than to jump

374
00:19:43,980 --> 00:19:46,565
when his uncle calls him
first thing in the morning.

375
00:19:46,566 --> 00:19:49,359
The Philly cops said Anna worked
for the federal government.

376
00:19:49,360 --> 00:19:50,652
Which part?

377
00:19:50,653 --> 00:19:53,071
Army Department, Human Resources,

378
00:19:53,072 --> 00:19:56,533
for some, uh, general or something.

379
00:19:56,534 --> 00:19:57,618
Must be a branch
of the Commanding General,

380
00:19:57,619 --> 00:20:01,622
U.S. Army, Human Resources Command.
Personnel department.

381
00:20:01,623 --> 00:20:03,206
Paperwork and records.

382
00:20:03,207 --> 00:20:04,499
Nothing exciting.

383
00:20:04,500 --> 00:20:06,585
It was exciting to her.

384
00:20:06,586 --> 00:20:09,004
<i>♪ Sit down, baby,
and have a little drink</i>...

385
00:20:09,005 --> 00:20:13,216
Getting out of the Jersey suburbs,
moving to D.C.

386
00:20:13,217 --> 00:20:16,386
<i>♪ Ain't no sense in you killing me...</i>

387
00:20:16,387 --> 00:20:18,805
I remember how proud she was when...

388
00:20:18,806 --> 00:20:22,768
<i>♪ Morning, noon and night ♪</i>

389
00:20:22,769 --> 00:20:24,227
<i>♪ Love you, pretty baby ♪</i>

390
00:20:24,228 --> 00:20:25,438
I'm sorry.

391
00:20:26,522 --> 00:20:28,066
<i>♪ Morning, noon and night</i>...

392
00:20:30,318 --> 00:20:31,819
We were both adopted.

393
00:20:34,656 --> 00:20:37,824
And we... we never fought.

394
00:20:37,825 --> 00:20:39,702
Not once.

395
00:20:40,995 --> 00:20:43,206
I always thought I was so lucky.

396
00:20:45,083 --> 00:20:49,753
You know, out of all the kids in the world
that needed families,

397
00:20:49,754 --> 00:20:52,089
I got her to be my sister.

398
00:20:52,090 --> 00:20:53,341
They're back.

399
00:20:55,760 --> 00:20:57,052
What? Who?

400
00:20:57,053 --> 00:20:59,179
The guys who came after me
for your sister's drive.

401
00:20:59,180 --> 00:21:02,140
Recognize them?

402
00:21:02,141 --> 00:21:04,226
<i>♪ Morning, noon and night ♪</i>

403
00:21:04,227 --> 00:21:05,311
No.

404
00:21:06,396 --> 00:21:09,231
<i>♪ Morning, noon and night ♪</i>

405
00:21:09,232 --> 00:21:11,024
<i>♪ Love you, pretty baby</i>...

406
00:21:11,025 --> 00:21:14,111
- 50 bucks for your jump drive.
- Why?

407
00:21:14,112 --> 00:21:15,362
You always ask questions

408
00:21:15,363 --> 00:21:17,030
when someone offers you
five times the value of something?

409
00:21:17,031 --> 00:21:18,199
[man] Okay, deal.

410
00:21:20,326 --> 00:21:21,619
What are you doing?

411
00:21:22,912 --> 00:21:24,413
Those guys were willing to come after me

412
00:21:24,414 --> 00:21:26,456
'cause they thought
Anna gave me something on the train.

413
00:21:26,457 --> 00:21:28,667
- I'm gonna find out why.
- Why do you care?

414
00:21:28,668 --> 00:21:30,794
[tense, dramatic music playing]

415
00:21:30,795 --> 00:21:32,379
Your sister's involved
in something big enough

416
00:21:32,380 --> 00:21:33,880
that the federal government's worried,

417
00:21:33,881 --> 00:21:36,550
and people are trying to work me over
on the street because of it.

418
00:21:36,551 --> 00:21:40,512
So big she saw no way out of it,
so she killed herself over it.

419
00:21:40,513 --> 00:21:42,139
I was the last person
that could've possibly helped her

420
00:21:42,140 --> 00:21:45,058
out of whatever situation
she was stuck in,

421
00:21:45,059 --> 00:21:47,019
and I didn't.

422
00:21:47,020 --> 00:21:49,479
- Then I'm coming.
- No. You're in uniform.

423
00:21:49,480 --> 00:21:51,189
You'll only complicate things.

424
00:21:51,190 --> 00:21:54,067
Stay here, keep trying
to get in touch with your nephew.

425
00:21:54,068 --> 00:21:55,652
He might know something.

426
00:21:55,653 --> 00:21:57,739
I'll be back in 90 minutes.

427
00:21:58,906 --> 00:22:01,199
[entry bells jingle]

428
00:22:01,200 --> 00:22:02,826
Look what I got.

429
00:22:02,827 --> 00:22:04,870
Your boss wants it,
and I want some money and answers.

430
00:22:04,871 --> 00:22:07,080
You can set up a meeting,
or you can try and take it,

431
00:22:07,081 --> 00:22:08,623
and I can kick your asses again.

432
00:22:08,624 --> 00:22:11,251
Either option's fine with me.

433
00:22:11,252 --> 00:22:13,254
[♪ Curtis Mayfield sings "Pusherman"]

434
00:22:24,974 --> 00:22:26,933
Thank you for meeting with me.

435
00:22:26,934 --> 00:22:28,643
It was my idea.

436
00:22:28,644 --> 00:22:32,481
Yeah, but after my cousins
crossed the line with you,

437
00:22:32,482 --> 00:22:34,399
I lost any hope we'd ever speak.

438
00:22:34,400 --> 00:22:38,111
So, thank you
for not holding their behavior against me.

439
00:22:38,112 --> 00:22:41,239
They could be very protective
of me and their aunt.

440
00:22:41,240 --> 00:22:42,909
My mother, Amisha Hoth.

441
00:22:45,453 --> 00:22:46,746
I'm Lila.

442
00:22:47,538 --> 00:22:49,498
Reacher.

443
00:22:49,499 --> 00:22:51,083
Your mother?

444
00:22:51,084 --> 00:22:54,711
She had me at 19, and it's been
just the two of us since then.

445
00:22:54,712 --> 00:22:56,964
Does she want the jump drive, too,
or just you?

446
00:23:00,510 --> 00:23:02,720
Walk with me, I'll explain everything.

447
00:23:06,390 --> 00:23:08,142
[bicycle bell dings]

448
00:23:10,353 --> 00:23:12,605
Did you know they call this place
the City of Brotherly Love?

449
00:23:14,190 --> 00:23:15,774
I like that.

450
00:23:15,775 --> 00:23:18,611
Their fans throw snowballs at Santa Claus.

451
00:23:20,363 --> 00:23:21,781
I don't understand.

452
00:23:22,740 --> 00:23:23,866
It doesn't matter.

453
00:23:25,118 --> 00:23:26,660
Look, I just want to know what's going on.

454
00:23:26,661 --> 00:23:28,787
<i>♪ Bad machine, super cool, super mean</i>...

455
00:23:28,788 --> 00:23:31,624
Oh. Best I start at the beginning.

456
00:23:32,625 --> 00:23:35,669
In the '90s,
my mother met a U.S. soldier named John.

457
00:23:35,670 --> 00:23:38,004
He was in Delta Force.

458
00:23:38,005 --> 00:23:41,049
She never learned his last name,
but they had an affair,

459
00:23:41,050 --> 00:23:43,177
and you're basically walking
with the result of that union.

460
00:23:44,887 --> 00:23:46,347
Okay.

461
00:23:48,182 --> 00:23:51,726
You're also walking
with a very sick person.

462
00:23:51,727 --> 00:23:53,729
I have leukemia.

463
00:23:55,356 --> 00:23:56,857
The bruises on your arm?

464
00:23:56,858 --> 00:23:59,442
My doctors say
I need a bone marrow transplant,

465
00:23:59,443 --> 00:24:01,361
but matches are hard to come by.

466
00:24:01,362 --> 00:24:04,072
And when you're mixed-race,
well, it's near impossible.

467
00:24:04,073 --> 00:24:06,449
What about your mother?

468
00:24:06,450 --> 00:24:09,078
Kidney disease, so, she can't donate.

469
00:24:10,121 --> 00:24:11,663
So your only hope is finding John?

470
00:24:11,664 --> 00:24:13,124
Yeah.

471
00:24:14,125 --> 00:24:17,460
I only know
he was stationed in Indonesia in '98.

472
00:24:17,461 --> 00:24:21,548
So I've contacted the Army,
veterans groups, military subreddits.

473
00:24:21,549 --> 00:24:23,176
Nothing worked.

474
00:24:24,427 --> 00:24:28,430
I eventually called the Pentagon, but just
got passed from department to department

475
00:24:28,431 --> 00:24:31,016
until I finally got in touch
with the most wonderful woman

476
00:24:31,017 --> 00:24:32,767
who worked in human resources.

477
00:24:32,768 --> 00:24:34,477
Anna Merrick.

478
00:24:34,478 --> 00:24:37,022
She was adopted,
so she understood the frustration

479
00:24:37,023 --> 00:24:39,608
of not knowing one's
full biological history.

480
00:24:39,609 --> 00:24:42,820
She worried if her son ever got sick,
she wouldn't know enough to help him.

481
00:24:43,905 --> 00:24:46,490
She promised to do all she could
to find my father, and she did.

482
00:24:47,575 --> 00:24:48,743
Someone important.

483
00:24:50,912 --> 00:24:52,913
You've looked on the drive?

484
00:24:52,914 --> 00:24:55,373
No, but the Feds wouldn't have been
asking about it

485
00:24:55,374 --> 00:24:57,460
unless it was someone important.

486
00:24:58,544 --> 00:25:00,712
Congressman John Sampson,

487
00:25:00,713 --> 00:25:02,631
Representative from North Carolina.

488
00:25:02,632 --> 00:25:04,216
You know him?

489
00:25:04,217 --> 00:25:07,094
Not really. Seen his name in the news.

490
00:25:07,845 --> 00:25:10,639
The frontrunner
to be his party's presidential nominee.

491
00:25:10,640 --> 00:25:11,973
And his odds are good.

492
00:25:11,974 --> 00:25:15,477
Let me guess. He didn't want to talk.

493
00:25:15,478 --> 00:25:17,103
None of my messages were returned.

494
00:25:17,104 --> 00:25:19,356
So you're gonna blackmail him
with information

495
00:25:19,357 --> 00:25:21,192
Anna stole for you from the Pentagon.

496
00:25:22,610 --> 00:25:25,654
- That's what's on the drive.
- I know it's distasteful,

497
00:25:25,655 --> 00:25:27,739
but I'm 29 years old, Reacher.

498
00:25:27,740 --> 00:25:29,532
I don't want to die.

499
00:25:29,533 --> 00:25:32,827
Anna had documents proving Sampson
was in Indonesia when I was conceived.

500
00:25:32,828 --> 00:25:35,372
And along with my mother's testimony,

501
00:25:35,373 --> 00:25:37,958
well, I'm hoping
that creates enough public pressure

502
00:25:37,959 --> 00:25:39,793
to get him to take a DNA test.

503
00:25:39,794 --> 00:25:43,713
And if he's a match,
it would be political suicide

504
00:25:43,714 --> 00:25:46,049
for him not to donate his bone marrow
to his own child, right?

505
00:25:46,050 --> 00:25:49,177
Why did Anna want to meet in Philadelphia?

506
00:25:49,178 --> 00:25:51,179
She said D.C. was the most surveilled city
in the world,

507
00:25:51,180 --> 00:25:54,307
and technically, she was committing
espionage by giving me these files.

508
00:25:54,308 --> 00:25:57,352
I was supposed to meet her at my hotel,
but, of course, she never showed up.

509
00:25:57,353 --> 00:25:59,188
She killed herself.

510
00:26:00,523 --> 00:26:02,732
Yeah. I saw the news.

511
00:26:02,733 --> 00:26:04,235
Why?

512
00:26:05,569 --> 00:26:06,946
[exhales] I wish I knew.

513
00:26:07,613 --> 00:26:10,241
But it kills me if what I asked of her
somehow led to it.

514
00:26:11,325 --> 00:26:14,662
Look, um, I don't have much...

515
00:26:16,038 --> 00:26:17,790
...but you can have all of it
for the drive.

516
00:26:19,875 --> 00:26:21,876
I'm sorry to get your hopes up.

517
00:26:21,877 --> 00:26:23,296
There's nothing on this.

518
00:26:34,849 --> 00:26:36,559
[man speaking Indonesian]

519
00:26:36,976 --> 00:26:38,394
It's nothing but sketches.

520
00:26:38,602 --> 00:26:39,770
Check again.

521
00:26:40,104 --> 00:26:41,688
I got it from an art student.

522
00:26:41,689 --> 00:26:43,482
That's all there is.

523
00:26:44,483 --> 00:26:46,651
I didn't know your situation.

524
00:26:46,652 --> 00:26:48,445
I'm a former investigator.

525
00:26:48,446 --> 00:26:50,363
It's my nature to want information.

526
00:26:50,364 --> 00:26:52,699
[gentle music playing]

527
00:26:52,700 --> 00:26:55,076
Then the police must have the drive.

528
00:26:55,077 --> 00:26:56,369
Maybe.

529
00:26:56,370 --> 00:26:58,121
But if they did,
the Feds wouldn't have been asking

530
00:26:58,122 --> 00:26:59,373
if Anna gave it to me this morning.

531
00:27:00,958 --> 00:27:02,542
Yeah, I-I understand.

532
00:27:02,543 --> 00:27:06,421
I'm-I'm staying at The Carter Hotel
on 10th Street

533
00:27:06,422 --> 00:27:08,923
until Anna's funeral is arranged.

534
00:27:08,924 --> 00:27:11,634
I mean, she was a friend
who died helping me.

535
00:27:11,635 --> 00:27:13,678
It's the least I could do.

536
00:27:13,679 --> 00:27:17,098
Here's my cell and my room number.

537
00:27:17,099 --> 00:27:19,101
Uh, thumb drive... [speaks Indonesian]

538
00:27:22,772 --> 00:27:26,483
If you find anything, please.

539
00:27:26,484 --> 00:27:28,611
What makes you think I'll keep looking?

540
00:27:29,945 --> 00:27:32,614
Like you said,
you're a former investigator.

541
00:27:32,615 --> 00:27:35,076
It's in your nature to want information.

542
00:27:38,704 --> 00:27:39,872
Good luck, Lila.

543
00:27:48,672 --> 00:27:50,298
- [♪ Old 97's play "Stoned"]
- <i>♪ Well, I must have been stoned ♪</i>

544
00:27:50,299 --> 00:27:51,591
[bell dings]

545
00:27:51,592 --> 00:27:53,343
<i>♪ When this whole thing started ♪</i>

546
00:27:53,344 --> 00:27:55,346
- [door opens]
- [entry bells jingle]

547
00:27:58,057 --> 00:28:02,977
<i>♪ 'Cause I just can't seem
to think straight anymore...</i>

548
00:28:02,978 --> 00:28:04,604
I gave her $40.

549
00:28:04,605 --> 00:28:07,732
It's been over an hour.
She-she was getting pissed.

550
00:28:07,733 --> 00:28:09,484
How'd your nephew take the news?

551
00:28:09,485 --> 00:28:11,195
I didn't track him down yet.

552
00:28:12,196 --> 00:28:14,155
What about you? What about...
did you find anything?

553
00:28:14,156 --> 00:28:17,409
Yeah. Seems your sister
may have stolen some confidential

554
00:28:17,410 --> 00:28:19,661
albeit pretty benign data
about Congressman John Sampson

555
00:28:19,662 --> 00:28:21,580
to help a woman prove he was her father.

556
00:28:22,498 --> 00:28:23,582
What else?

557
00:28:24,250 --> 00:28:25,459
That's it.

558
00:28:27,837 --> 00:28:29,254
John Sampson?

559
00:28:29,255 --> 00:28:30,714
John Sampson.

560
00:28:32,466 --> 00:28:34,176
Where you going?

561
00:28:35,344 --> 00:28:37,805
I'm gonna find out all I can
about John Sampson.

562
00:28:38,889 --> 00:28:42,809
Not my business, but the federal
government's looking into this.

563
00:28:42,810 --> 00:28:45,687
What do you think a solo cop
from a small town in Jersey's gonna do?

564
00:28:45,688 --> 00:28:47,440
I have no idea.

565
00:28:48,607 --> 00:28:52,236
But my sister died because she was trying
to help somebody find their father.

566
00:28:53,237 --> 00:28:55,738
That is exactly who Anna was.

567
00:28:55,739 --> 00:28:59,243
She never let anyone down,
and I'm not gonna let her down.

568
00:29:03,122 --> 00:29:04,873
Okay.

569
00:29:04,874 --> 00:29:06,500
Let's go.

570
00:29:07,751 --> 00:29:09,002
You're coming?

571
00:29:09,003 --> 00:29:10,296
[bell dings]

572
00:29:12,047 --> 00:29:14,758
I'm good at not letting people
get away with things.

573
00:29:16,635 --> 00:29:18,678
[entry bells jingle]

574
00:29:18,679 --> 00:29:21,307
[indistinct chatter]

575
00:29:26,812 --> 00:29:28,563
Hey! What are you doing?

576
00:29:28,564 --> 00:29:30,191
Taking our files.

577
00:29:30,983 --> 00:29:32,108
Your files are in D.C.

578
00:29:32,109 --> 00:29:34,110
- My desk is right here.
- Easy, killer.

579
00:29:34,111 --> 00:29:36,112
They're taking over
the Anna Merrick suicide.

580
00:29:36,113 --> 00:29:38,990
What's there to take over?
The case ended when the woman died.

581
00:29:38,991 --> 00:29:40,909
The files, please, Detective.

582
00:29:40,910 --> 00:29:42,745
The captain signed off on it.

583
00:29:53,964 --> 00:29:56,509
- Assholes.
- [Docherty] Why the hell do you care?

584
00:29:57,593 --> 00:30:00,178
Machine fucked you sideways,
you still didn't learn?

585
00:30:00,179 --> 00:30:03,723
Punch in, punch out,
hope the phone doesn't ring in between.

586
00:30:03,724 --> 00:30:06,017
It's about the income, not the outcome.

587
00:30:06,018 --> 00:30:07,852
I know.

588
00:30:07,853 --> 00:30:09,271
But still.

589
00:30:10,272 --> 00:30:12,274
Touching my shit.

590
00:30:16,529 --> 00:30:17,862
[Jacob] <i>No scandals,</i>

591
00:30:17,863 --> 00:30:19,822
no black marks.

592
00:30:19,823 --> 00:30:22,450
No politician's this clean.

593
00:30:22,451 --> 00:30:23,911
[printer whirring]

594
00:30:24,995 --> 00:30:27,330
Why are you printing them?

595
00:30:27,331 --> 00:30:30,041
It's information on the guy.

596
00:30:30,042 --> 00:30:32,919
It's superficial horseshit.

597
00:30:32,920 --> 00:30:35,672
It... it's background.

598
00:30:35,673 --> 00:30:37,924
I need his military data.

599
00:30:37,925 --> 00:30:40,885
Delta Force records would be confidential,
but there could be government documents

600
00:30:40,886 --> 00:30:44,556
that speak generally to overseas missions
Sampson might have been involved in.

601
00:30:44,557 --> 00:30:46,976
Could shed some light on who this guy is.

602
00:30:48,644 --> 00:30:49,770
Excuse me.

603
00:30:50,604 --> 00:30:52,188
Where are your military archives?

604
00:30:52,189 --> 00:30:54,817
Oh, like old stuff?
Old stuff's in the basement.

605
00:30:56,569 --> 00:30:59,530
Guess I'm headed to the basement
to look for "old stuff."

606
00:31:12,167 --> 00:31:13,377
[light clicks]

607
00:31:20,426 --> 00:31:21,885
[light clicks]

608
00:31:24,513 --> 00:31:26,015
[light clicks]

609
00:31:30,185 --> 00:31:31,520
[light clicks]

610
00:31:46,201 --> 00:31:47,453
[grunts softly]

611
00:32:10,976 --> 00:32:12,978
- [eerie music playing]
- [light clicks]

612
00:32:15,314 --> 00:32:16,690
[light clicks]

613
00:32:19,026 --> 00:32:20,861
[man] Give me the drive.

614
00:32:22,571 --> 00:32:23,739
No.

615
00:32:28,077 --> 00:32:29,536
- [screams]
- [exciting music playing]

616
00:32:44,885 --> 00:32:46,553
[groans]

617
00:32:48,138 --> 00:32:50,140
[both grunting]

618
00:32:56,063 --> 00:32:57,773
[shouts]

619
00:33:00,859 --> 00:33:02,443
[glass shatters]

620
00:33:02,444 --> 00:33:03,821
[groans]

621
00:33:10,327 --> 00:33:12,204
[straining]

622
00:33:16,875 --> 00:33:18,043
[screams]

623
00:33:20,212 --> 00:33:22,715
- [clicking]
- [man groaning]

624
00:33:30,514 --> 00:33:31,931
[knife thuds]

625
00:33:31,932 --> 00:33:33,767
[panting]

626
00:33:35,936 --> 00:33:37,730
[steam hissing]

627
00:33:38,731 --> 00:33:40,357
[body squeaking on floor]

628
00:33:45,571 --> 00:33:47,573
[suspenseful music playing]

629
00:34:01,378 --> 00:34:02,837
We need to go.

630
00:34:02,838 --> 00:34:04,465
Lila Hoth's in trouble.

631
00:34:11,680 --> 00:34:14,140
- [siren wailing]
- [Jacob] A coal bin?

632
00:34:14,141 --> 00:34:17,059
- I'm a cop! You can't do shit like that.
- Shut up.

633
00:34:17,060 --> 00:34:18,644
[automated voice] <i>Please leave
a message after the tone.</i>

634
00:34:18,645 --> 00:34:20,563
- [beeps]
- Lila, it's Reacher.

635
00:34:20,564 --> 00:34:22,398
Someone just took a run at me,
they might be coming after you.

636
00:34:22,399 --> 00:34:24,026
Get out of your hotel.

637
00:34:26,111 --> 00:34:28,447
- How much further?
- A couple minutes.

638
00:34:29,865 --> 00:34:31,574
- I'm calling again.
- [beeps]

639
00:34:31,575 --> 00:34:32,784
[line ringing]

640
00:34:32,785 --> 00:34:34,786
- [shower running]
- [phone buzzing]

641
00:34:34,787 --> 00:34:36,789
♪ ♪

642
00:34:53,889 --> 00:34:55,056
[siren wailing]

643
00:34:55,057 --> 00:34:56,557
[tires screech]

644
00:34:56,558 --> 00:34:58,560
[exciting music playing]

645
00:35:02,022 --> 00:35:03,649
[people murmuring]

646
00:35:06,360 --> 00:35:08,319
- They're at the top floors.
- Room 506.

647
00:35:08,320 --> 00:35:10,029
Take the west stairs.

648
00:35:10,030 --> 00:35:12,032
♪ ♪

649
00:35:21,583 --> 00:35:23,167
[trilling]

650
00:35:23,168 --> 00:35:24,378
[lock clicks]

651
00:35:39,434 --> 00:35:41,227
[shouts]

652
00:35:41,228 --> 00:35:42,229
Lila!

653
00:35:43,272 --> 00:35:44,522
Stay there!

654
00:35:44,523 --> 00:35:45,899
[grunts]

655
00:35:47,442 --> 00:35:49,236
[both grunting]

656
00:35:50,946 --> 00:35:52,447
[shouts]

657
00:35:54,575 --> 00:35:56,785
[shouting]

658
00:35:58,161 --> 00:35:59,872
[grunting]

659
00:36:03,667 --> 00:36:05,502
[distant thudding]

660
00:36:07,546 --> 00:36:09,339
[shouting]

661
00:36:10,257 --> 00:36:11,717
[Lila screams]

662
00:36:14,928 --> 00:36:16,805
[both grunting]

663
00:36:23,061 --> 00:36:24,771
[choking]

664
00:36:30,652 --> 00:36:31,904
[grunts]

665
00:36:34,740 --> 00:36:36,617
[shouts]

666
00:36:40,412 --> 00:36:41,621
[door opens]

667
00:36:41,622 --> 00:36:43,789
[tense music playing]

668
00:36:43,790 --> 00:36:45,584
[exhales]

669
00:36:52,090 --> 00:36:54,009
♪ ♪

670
00:36:55,010 --> 00:36:56,136
Oh, shit.

671
00:36:59,514 --> 00:37:00,599
What?

672
00:37:06,939 --> 00:37:07,940
Get back inside.

673
00:37:09,900 --> 00:37:11,902
[screams]

674
00:37:14,488 --> 00:37:15,863
[vehicle doors closing]

675
00:37:15,864 --> 00:37:17,490
[tires squealing]

676
00:37:17,491 --> 00:37:18,950
You okay?

677
00:37:18,951 --> 00:37:20,368
I think so.

678
00:37:20,369 --> 00:37:21,536
[door thuds]

679
00:37:24,957 --> 00:37:26,625
I think my nose is broken.

680
00:37:27,292 --> 00:37:28,709
Cops will be here any minute.

681
00:37:28,710 --> 00:37:30,753
- Let's move.
- But we're the good guys.

682
00:37:30,754 --> 00:37:32,630
You want to answer questions
about why you're here?

683
00:37:32,631 --> 00:37:35,174
Blackmailing's a crime
no matter how good the reason.

684
00:37:35,175 --> 00:37:36,343
[grunts]

685
00:37:37,427 --> 00:37:38,886
- Where's your family?
- Getting food.

686
00:37:38,887 --> 00:37:40,930
I'll call and tell them
not to come back here.

687
00:37:40,931 --> 00:37:44,141
Do it after you get dressed.
We got to move, now.

688
00:37:44,142 --> 00:37:46,227
- [suspenseful music playing]
- [siren wailing]

689
00:37:46,228 --> 00:37:48,230
[engine revving]

690
00:37:51,400 --> 00:37:53,275
Keep your heads down.

691
00:37:53,276 --> 00:37:55,278
- Lose the washcloth.
- It's bleeding.

692
00:37:57,114 --> 00:37:58,364
[sirens wailing]

693
00:37:58,365 --> 00:37:59,783
Menus.

694
00:38:02,244 --> 00:38:04,246
[tires screeching]

695
00:38:07,416 --> 00:38:09,000
There'll be more.

696
00:38:09,001 --> 00:38:10,751
Hotel staff will be able to describe you.

697
00:38:10,752 --> 00:38:13,087
Go buy new clothes.
Grays, dark blues, muted colors.

698
00:38:13,088 --> 00:38:14,296
Why?

699
00:38:14,297 --> 00:38:16,048
'Cause you don't want to stand out.

700
00:38:16,049 --> 00:38:18,467
And a woman like you
stands out enough as it is.

701
00:38:18,468 --> 00:38:20,428
Go see your mom.

702
00:38:20,429 --> 00:38:22,471
Get a shit-box, all-cash motel.

703
00:38:22,472 --> 00:38:24,807
Won't be nice, but it'll be safe.

704
00:38:24,808 --> 00:38:26,142
Where are you going?

705
00:38:26,143 --> 00:38:27,394
I got to go see a cop.

706
00:38:34,860 --> 00:38:36,153
[silverware dropping]

707
00:38:37,446 --> 00:38:39,989
[Jacob] No, no, that's fine. It is.
Okay, all right.

708
00:38:39,990 --> 00:38:41,949
When he gets back,
you got to have him call me.

709
00:38:41,950 --> 00:38:44,453
This is really, really important, okay?

710
00:38:45,746 --> 00:38:46,788
Thank you.

711
00:38:48,707 --> 00:38:50,750
One of Ben's frat brothers

712
00:38:50,751 --> 00:38:53,461
says a day and a half ago,
he left a bar with some gorgeous woman

713
00:38:53,462 --> 00:38:55,172
and he hasn't returned to campus yet.

714
00:38:56,840 --> 00:38:58,883
I'm gonna let him have his fun.

715
00:38:58,884 --> 00:39:01,010
His whole world's about
to get rocked soon when I talk to him.

716
00:39:01,011 --> 00:39:03,305
- Or when he sees it on the news.
- There she is.

717
00:39:04,431 --> 00:39:06,599
[scoffs] What the fuck now?

718
00:39:06,600 --> 00:39:09,478
- You were waiting for me?
- Need to ask you something.

719
00:39:10,562 --> 00:39:12,188
Detective Tamara Green.

720
00:39:12,189 --> 00:39:13,522
Sorry about your sister.

721
00:39:13,523 --> 00:39:15,524
Look, I just punched out.

722
00:39:15,525 --> 00:39:17,943
You should've come in when I was still
getting paid to deal with the public.

723
00:39:17,944 --> 00:39:19,987
Wouldn't have. Docherty doesn't like me.

724
00:39:19,988 --> 00:39:21,655
Would've talked you out
of doing me a favor.

725
00:39:21,656 --> 00:39:24,492
That old man couldn't talk me out of shit.

726
00:39:24,493 --> 00:39:25,910
What do you want?

727
00:39:25,911 --> 00:39:27,787
Has a jump drive turned up
since I left this morning?

728
00:39:27,788 --> 00:39:29,164
Now you sound like the Feds.

729
00:39:30,082 --> 00:39:31,248
They were looking for it, too.

730
00:39:31,249 --> 00:39:32,458
First thing they asked for.

731
00:39:32,459 --> 00:39:34,376
I'm-a tell you the same thing I told them.

732
00:39:34,377 --> 00:39:37,171
I inventoried all the evidence and
personal effects before they got there,

733
00:39:37,172 --> 00:39:38,589
and there was no flash drive.

734
00:39:38,590 --> 00:39:40,258
[dark music playing]

735
00:39:41,635 --> 00:39:43,052
Why? What's on it?

736
00:39:43,053 --> 00:39:44,261
Don't know.

737
00:39:44,262 --> 00:39:46,263
But people think Anna Merrick gave me one.

738
00:39:46,264 --> 00:39:47,723
Two people tried to kill me over it.

739
00:39:47,724 --> 00:39:48,974
Since I saw you this morning?

740
00:39:48,975 --> 00:39:50,351
Busy day.

741
00:39:50,352 --> 00:39:52,144
One came at me at a library,
one at a hotel.

742
00:39:52,145 --> 00:39:54,106
What happened to those people?

743
00:39:55,690 --> 00:39:56,899
They got away.

744
00:39:56,900 --> 00:39:58,984
Is there any chance
that the Feds maybe said

745
00:39:58,985 --> 00:40:00,611
- anything about why they--
- Look,

746
00:40:00,612 --> 00:40:03,030
all I know is a woman killed herself
on one of my subways.

747
00:40:03,031 --> 00:40:05,574
She was important enough that the Feds
officially took over the matter.

748
00:40:05,575 --> 00:40:08,119
Now you say people coming after you
for some jump drive?

749
00:40:08,120 --> 00:40:10,246
And I think you may have done
some bad shit to them.

750
00:40:10,247 --> 00:40:12,832
Like I said, they got away.

751
00:40:12,833 --> 00:40:14,750
Your knuckle's all jacked up,
your face is bruised

752
00:40:14,751 --> 00:40:17,545
and you got dry blood behind your ear.

753
00:40:17,546 --> 00:40:19,256
Blood's not mine.

754
00:40:20,966 --> 00:40:23,926
Know what? Not my monkeys, not my circus.

755
00:40:23,927 --> 00:40:26,595
I deal with OD'ing junkies
and Wawa smash-and-grabs.

756
00:40:26,596 --> 00:40:29,348
You want to run around
like a cowboy, go ahead.

757
00:40:29,349 --> 00:40:31,559
I'm-a run out five years
and collect my pension.

758
00:40:31,560 --> 00:40:33,519
I'm coming off a double night.

759
00:40:33,520 --> 00:40:36,939
I'm gonna head home,
watch <i>SportsCenter</i> and hit the sack.

760
00:40:36,940 --> 00:40:39,775
You need extra help? You got Barney Fife.

761
00:40:39,776 --> 00:40:41,570
Once again, sorry for your loss.

762
00:40:43,947 --> 00:40:45,573
Now what?

763
00:40:45,574 --> 00:40:47,449
We talk to Sampson.

764
00:40:47,450 --> 00:40:49,785
- You're driving.
- To D.C.?

765
00:40:49,786 --> 00:40:52,538
- Just a day or two.
- No, no, I can't take the cruiser.

766
00:40:52,539 --> 00:40:54,373
I'll get in trouble.

767
00:40:54,374 --> 00:40:56,167
I got to bring it back to Jersey.

768
00:40:56,168 --> 00:41:00,462
There's a 6:00 p.m. train to D.C.
every night from Penn Station.

769
